      "id": 1,
      "text": "Farmersville got its name from settlers William Pickney Chapman and John Hendrex, who chose it to reflect the chief occupation of many early residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farmersville%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1885, the First National Bank and the Farmersville Times began operation, and the bank stayed Collin County’s oldest independent bank through 1988.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/farmersville-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "An earlier settlement called Sugar Hill started in 1845 but was abandoned when its residents moved to Farmersville in 1854.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/farmersville-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In its early days, Farmersville served as a major railroad shipping point for cattle and bois d'arc posts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/farmersville-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The First Baptist Church of Farmersville was organized on May 14, 1865, marking the town’s first organized religious community.",
